A1. If you're blind, you just can't do it. ~ SEE
A4. Metallically speaking, this one is a mix-up. ~ AMALGAM
A8. What the U. S. used to send Germany every other day. ~ ULTIMATUM
A10. Just one. ~ AN
A14. An abbreviation in the metric system. ~ MM
A16. Classic literature made famous by kidney pills. ~ ALMANAC
A20. The one that makes home worth living in. ~ MA
A21. An overgrown snake that isn't our idea of a pet. ~ BOA
A22. A period of time. ~ ERA
A23. ——, this is Paris! ~ SO
A25. Where Leopold and Loeb are going to stay longer, and perhaps learn more than they did in college. ~ PRISON
A27. What you'll be if you go canoeing on the ocean. ~ TOSSED
A29. The same. (You'd better try the verticals.) ~ IDEM
A30. What your wife thinks you are when you pull that one about sitting up with a sick friend ~ LIAR
A31. In ——, thy name is woman. ~ CONSISTENCY
A33. A shavetail abbreviated. ~ LT
A34. All's well. ~ OK
A35. What a Westerner does with a "mean pistol." ~ TOTES
A39. Highbrow for "doors." ~ PORTALS
A40. If you get this. You just can't control yourself. ~ PALSY
A41. The greatest man in American history, so the Southern boys tell us. ~ LEE
A42. Came before Lincoln (not Ford). ~ ABE
A45. A statement which appears to be contradictory. ~ PARADOX
A49. People entirely surrounded by water. ~ ISLANDERS
A51. A kind of rum. ~ BAY
A52. Where African golfers came from (abr.). ~ AF
A53. "To be"—not, "not to be." ~ IS
A55. More properly "limb." ~ LEG
A57. The nearest some girls come to cooking is making this in traffic. ~ JAM
A59. For the sake of which Earl Carroll went to jail. ~ ART
A60. One on Main street every Decoration Day. ~ PARADE
A62. Most modern girls are noticeable for their lack of it. ~ ATTIRE
A65. Good for rheumatism if you can stand the smell. ~ ARNICA
A66. Otherwise known as a "cad." ~ ROTTER
A67. Washday (abr.). ~ MON
A68. A long-eared animal, or a dumb human. ~ ASS
A69. A kiddy car. ~ SCOOTER
A74. Pertaining to the sense of hearing. ~ AURAL
A76. An Italian river. ~ PO
A78. Hepatica's given name. ~ SAL
A79. What the turkey gets for Christmas. ~ AX
A80. 'Tis to laugh. ~ HA
A82. This is it. ~ IT
A83. Where Silas stores the fodder. (Pl.) ~ SILOS
A86. A marsh—very poor for real estate purposes. ~ FEN

D1. What you do over the deep blue sea. ~ SAIL
D2. A kind of a tree (not a family tree). ~ ELM
D3. A deucedly English exclamation. ~ EGAD
D4. You know me ——. ~ AL
D5. An abbreviation for a full grown hill. ~ MT
D6. A preposition of place. ~ AT
D7. Twelfth letter in the Greek alphabet—ask the man who owns a restaurant. ~ MU
D8. If you just must be cremated they'll put your remains in one of these. ~ URN
D9. The woman has yet to be born who can keep this way. ~ MUM
D10. A fungus of the mushroom family. Shades of Webster! ~ AGARIC
D11. What a flower does in the spring, tra-la. ~ BLOOMS
D12. An exclamation of glee. ~ HA
D13. What any inmate of Sing Sing would rather have than ice cream. ~ PAROLE
D15. What loves company? ~ MISERY
D16. Don't ever get this way without leave if you're in the Army or married. ~ ABSENT
D17. The flapper's delight. ~ MAN
D18. What bobbed hair has done away with. ~ NET
D19. A game of cards, or a pavilion— my, my, what variety. ~ CASINO
D20. This is too easy—Member of Parliament (abr.). ~ MP
D24. The color of U. S. Army misfit uniforms (abr.). ~ OD
D26. Some people worship these, some use them for incense burners. ~ IDOLS
D28. Potatoes come in them. ~ SACKS
D32. Often the easiest thing to do in a breach of promise suit. ~ SETTLE
D35. A good part of the Woolworth Building not to fall off of. ~ TOP
D36. Pertaining to the mouth. ~ ORAL
D37. What the family doc tries to do to your pains. ~ EASE
D38. Foxes are noted for being this way. ~ SLY
D42. The kind of a sheik who has lots of sand (that lets the drug store variety out). ~ ARAB
D43. Yes, we haven't a single one. ~ BANANA
D44. A circling current of water. ~ EDDY
D45. Often seen at the end of a letter—not, please remit (abr.). ~ PS
D46. New York was all for ——. ~ AL
D47. Old English (abr.). ~ OE
D48. "XR"—(We don't know what this means ourselves!). ~ XR
D49. A place where the stokers don't get paid. ~ INFERNO
D50. One way to keep them up is to pour them down. ~ SPIRITS
D52. A fireman doesn't like a false one. (Pl.). ~ ALARMS
D54. Funk & Wagnalls say "special weight, importance, or significance." ~ STRESS
D56. Profits. ~ GAINS
D57. The way you get when another fellow calls on the sweet woman. ~ JEALOUS
D58. Most men's troubles come under this heading. ~ MARITAL
D59. This one is very sweet—the oil from rose petals. ~ ATTAR
D60. The old man. ~ PA
D61. Abbreviation for the district where everything is being investigated. ~ DC
D63. Midway between go and hell. ~ TO
D64. A comparative ending. ~ ER
D70. The abbreviated form of calcium. (Come on, you chemist.) ~ CA
D71. A prophet. ~ ORACLE
D72. [no clue printed] ~ EL
D73. One way to go in an elevator. ~ UP
D75. A domestic bovine quadruped. ~ OX
D77. An exclamation of surprise. ~ OH
D79. See No. 6 vertical. ~ AT
D81. In like manner. (Adverb.) ~ AS
D82. Third person singular present indicative of the verb "be." ~ IS
D84. On the condition. (Conj.) ~ IF
D85. What you aren't when you're off. ~ ON


